Scott’s computable presentation of a neighbourhood system is an enumeration \(D=\{X_n\mid n\in N\}\) such that the ternary equality \(X_n\cap X_m=X_k\) and the binary consistency statement \(\exists k.\, X_k\subseteq X_n\) and \(X_k\subseteq X_m\) are recursively decidable; the sequence is the presentation, inclusion of neighbourhoods is then decidable because \(X_n\subseteq X_m\) if and only if the intersection equals \(X_n\), and Gödel numbers of the two relations are demanded only in the stricter reading that handles infinitely many domains at once. `ComputablePresentation` carries the enumeration `X` with `mem_X` and `surj`, and its fields `interEq_computable` and `cons_computable` are those two relations, but `RecDecidable` is the existence of a primitive-recursive characteristic function, and the structure additionally stores a primitive-recursive intersection operation `inter`, specified by `inter_spec` on consistent indices, together with a chosen `masterIdx` of the master neighbourhood. Scott recovers an intersection index by searching with (i), so a presentation whose intersection function is only general recursive is a presentation in the text and not a `ComputablePresentation`. `IsEffectivelyGiven` is the existence of such a structure. `incl_computable` decides inclusion by sending the pair \((n,m)\) through `inclShuffle` to the triple \((n,m,n)\) and using intersection-equality; `eq_computable` conjoins that decider with the same decider after `swapPair`, which exchanges the two components of a pair code. `reindexInvolutive` transports the enumeration, both deciders, `inter`, and `masterIdx` along a primitive-recursive involution by conjugating codes and cancelling the involution twice. `unitPresentation` enumerates the one-point system by the constant full set, so both of Scott’s relations hold for every index and are decided by the constantly true decider, with `inter` and `masterIdx` equal to zero; `unitSys_isEffectivelyGiven` records that this is a computable presentation. **Theorem:** partial — `X`, `interEq_computable`, and `cons_computable` are the enumeration and Scott’s two relations, but decidability is primitive-recursive and `inter` together with `masterIdx` are data the definition does not require. **Proof:** yes — inclusion is the biconditional Scott records, obtained by `inclShuffle`, equality is mutual inclusion via `swapPair`, and the one-point system is the constant presentation.
